Tests first for code review findings **US-02-R1-F02** (and, ahead of the backend change, **R1-F01**); read `reviews/US-02-review-R1.md`. Branch `feat/us-02-plan-list`. Do not push or switch branches. Touch only `backend/tests/`. The frontend-developer works on `frontend/` in parallel; the orchestrator works on scripts and docs. Do not start background commands.

Context: a plan must have at least one coverage when it is published (US-02 spec, AC-09). Today a custom Document Service middleware (`backend/src/documents/plan-coverages.ts`) enforces it, but your test only covers the `create` path: removing `context.action === 'publish'` from the middleware keeps every test green. The human decided to replace the middleware with Strapi's own schema validation (`coverages` set to `required` together with `min: 1`; the reviewer verified in `@strapi/core` that Strapi then refuses an empty list on create, update and publish). The strapi-developer will make that change after you.

Write the tests so that they prove the **behaviour** through every path an editor or the API uses:
- create published with no coverages → refused (already there);
- **publish an existing draft** that has no coverages → refused (the normal editor path);
- **update a published plan to remove all its coverages** → refused;
- a draft may still be saved with no coverages, if the spec allows incomplete drafts (check spec §7.1 and AC-09; if the spec is silent, report it instead of assuming);
- a control case with a valid plan that succeeds, proving the test data is otherwise valid.

These must pass against the current middleware where it already covers the path, and against the schema-based rule after the change. Any path the middleware misses today should be red now; say which. Keep the test independent of the mechanism.

Run `cd backend && npm test` and report red and green with reasons. Commit (`test(backend): …`, `Resolves: US-02-R1-F02`, `Refs: US-02-AC-09`, Co-Authored-By trailer) with `git add <path> && git commit … -- <path>`. Report the SHA.

